{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update34.json\",\"summary\":\"Submitted the three-order probe that turns the empty-command question's ordering discriminator into a measurement. The previous revision finally applied the delivery-order parameter and its receipt (a21219a3837544dda2b538c1a5e641ec) shows both recorded orders - 2,4,3 and 3,4,2 - ending with the owner's instance holding an empty command, which refutes the simple last-reply-wins reading and pointed to the quorum trigger as the deciding moment. This revision adds the discriminating third order 3,2,4, in which the command-carrying reply is the second one delivered and therefore the last adopted before the dispatch, and local feedback already shows it behaving differently: the owner's instance keeps its command and the recovery re-proposes it, while the first two orders lose it. If the receipt confirms that, the same reply set has produced three outcomes differing only in arrival order, which locates the defect precisely: the value a recovery adopts is fixed by which reply is adopted last before the slow quorum is reached, so a network reordering alone decides whether the owner's command survives. The experiment declares the third order's prerequisite chain and a comparison expecting the command to be kept, keeping the two earlier comparisons unchanged; its receipt and the interpretation update - replacing the account's 'order variant unmeasured' limitation with the measured three-order result and the refined mechanism - remain outstanding, and the run's other accounts are untouched.\"}"}}
